<article class="env-lemma" id="05VB">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05VB">Lemma <span data-tag="05VB">18.39.2</span>.</a> Let $(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{O})$ be a ringed site. Let $p$ be a point of $\mathcal{C}$. If $\mathcal{F}$ is a flat $\mathcal{O}$-module, then $\mathcal{F}_ p$ is a flat $\mathcal{O}_ p$-module. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      In Section <a href="/tag/05V6" data-tag="05V6">18.37</a> we have seen that we can think of $p$ as a morphism of ringed topoi 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  (p, \text{id}_{\mathcal{O}_ p}) : (\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (pt), \mathcal{O}_ p) \longrightarrow (\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C}), \mathcal{O}).  \]
</div>
    <p>
       such that the pullback functor $p^* : \textit{Mod}(\mathcal{O}) \to \textit{Mod}(\mathcal{O}_ p)$ equals the stalk functor. Thus the lemma follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/05VD" data-tag="05VD">18.39.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>